.TH SDM 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sdm, sgreet \- Substrate display manager and graphical login greeter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/sbin/sdm
.PP
.B DISPLAY=:0 /usr/sbin/sgreet
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sdm
provides a graphical login on the framebuffer console.
It is a shell script that loops forever:
.IP 1. 3
remove
.I /tmp/.X0\-lock
and
.I /tmp/.X11\-unix/X0
and create
.IR /tmp/.X11\-unix ;
.IP 2. 3
start the X server in the background as
.B "Xfbdev \-ac \-retro \-noreset \-dumbSched vt1 :0"
with its output sent to
.IR /var/log/xlog.txt ;
.IP 3. 3
run
.B sgreet
in the foreground with
.B DISPLAY
set to
.BR :0 ;
.IP 4. 3
when
.B sgreet
exits, kill the X server, wait for it, pause one second and start over.
.PP
.B sdm
takes no arguments and does not put itself in the background.
It is started at boot by
.IR /etc/rc.d/60-sdm ,
which runs it in the background with output to
.IR /var/log/sdm.log ,
records its pid in
.I /var/run/sdm.pid
and does nothing if
.IR /usr/sbin/sdm ,
.I /usr/sbin/sgreet
or
.I /usr/bin/Xfbdev
is missing.
.SS The greeter
.B sgreet
is an Xlib client that runs as root.
It connects to
.B $DISPLAY
(default
.BR :0 ),
retrying for about 10 seconds while the server starts, and shows a
centred window with a
.IB host " login:"
field, a
.B Password:
field (echoed as asterisks), the selected session, and
.B Reboot
and
.B "Power off"
buttons.
It grabs the keyboard, since no window manager is running.
The host name comes from
.BR gethostname (2),
or from
.I /etc/hostname
if that returns an empty name,
.B localhost
or
.BR (none) .
It uses the font
.BR 9x15 ,
falling back to
.BR fixed .
.PP
Keys:
.TP
.B Return
In the user field, move to the password field; in the password field,
try to log in.
.TP
.B Tab
Move from the user field to the password field.
.TP
.BR BackSpace ", " Delete
Delete the last character of the current field.
.TP
.B Escape
Clear both fields.
.TP
.B F1
Select the next session from
.IR /etc/sdm/sessions .
.PP
Only printable ASCII characters are accepted; each field holds up to 127.
.PP
The password is checked with
.BR crypt (3)
against the user's entry in
.IR /etc/shadow ,
or, if there is none there, against the password field of
.I /etc/passwd
unless it begins with
.BR x .
An empty stored password accepts any password; a stored password beginning
with
.B *
or
.B !
is locked.
On failure the fields are cleared and
.B "Login incorrect"
is shown.
.PP
On success
.B sgreet
closes its display connection and forks.
The child sets its group, supplementary groups and user ID to the user's,
sets
.BR HOME ,
.B SHELL
(default
.IR /bin/sh ),
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR PATH ,
.BR DISPLAY ,
.B TERM=xterm
and
.BR LANG=en_US.UTF\-8 ,
sets
.B SDM_SESSION
to the selected session's command (or unsets it), changes to the home
directory (or
.IR / ),
calls
.BR setsid (2)
and runs
.BR "/bin/sh /etc/X11/Xsession" ,
or
.I /usr/bin/xterm
if that cannot be executed.
The parent waits for the session to end and exits 0, which makes
.B sdm
restart the X server and the greeter.
.PP
The
.B Reboot
and
.B "Power off"
buttons send
.B SIGUSR2
or
.B SIGUSR1
to
.BR init (8),
which shuts the system down cleanly; if the signal cannot be sent,
.B sgreet
calls
.BR reboot (2)
directly.
No password is needed to use them.
.SS Sessions
.I /etc/sdm/sessions
lists the sessions offered, one per line as
.IP
.IB label " = " command
.PP
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored, as are lines without
.B =
or with an empty label or command.
At most 16 sessions are read; the first is selected initially.
Labels are truncated to 63 characters and commands to 255.
If the file is missing or has no valid lines, a single session named
.B Default
with an empty command is offered.
.PP
.I /etc/X11/Xsession
runs as the user.
If
.I ~/.xsession
is executable, it is executed instead of everything else.
Otherwise the script starts
.B "xterm \-fn 9x15"
in the background (output to
.IR /var/log/xterm.txt )
and executes
.B $SDM_SESSION
(default
.BR matwm2 )
with output to
.IR /var/log/xsession.txt .
The session ends when that command exits.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B sdm
runs until it receives
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT ,
then exits 0.
.PP
.B sgreet
exits 0 after a session ends, and 1 if it cannot open the display or
load a font.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
.B sdm
kills the X server it started and exits 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
Display
.B sgreet
connects to; default
.BR :0 .
.TP
.B SDM_SESSION
Set by
.B sgreet
for the session; the command
.I /etc/X11/Xsession
executes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/sdm
The supervisor script.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/sgreet
The greeter.
.TP
.I /etc/sdm/sessions
Session list.
.TP
.I /etc/X11/Xsession
Session start script.
.TP
.I /etc/X11/starttde\-substrate
Session script for the Trinity desktop, listed in the default sessions file.
.TP
.I ~/.xsession
Per-user session script, used if executable.
.TP
.IR /etc/passwd ", " /etc/shadow
Account and password data.
.TP
.I /etc/rc.d/60\-sdm
Boot script.
.TP
.IR /var/log/xlog.txt ", " /var/log/sdm.log
X server and
.B sdm
output.
.SH EXAMPLE
A sessions file offering twm and an xterm-only session:
.EX
twm   = twm
xterm = xterm \-fn 9x15
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR login (1),
.BR init (8),
.BR reboot (8),
.BR poweroff (8),
.BR getty (8),
.BR crypt (3)
.SH BUGS
The X server runs with access control disabled
.RB ( \-ac ),
so any client able to reach display :0 can connect to it.
.PP
.BR init (8)
always runs a getty on
.IR /dev/tty1 ,
which shares the console with the X server.
.PP
No utmp or wtmp records are written for graphical logins.
.PP
.B sgreet
takes a single character per key press and cannot enter non-ASCII text.
.PP
Because
.B sdm
waits for
.B sgreet
in the foreground, the shell delivers
.B SIGTERM
to it only after the greeter or session exits.
